{"term": "فتاة الشرف", "lang": "ar", "script": "arab", "romanization": "fatāt al-sharaf", "category": "honor_shame_economy", "subcategory": "female_chastity", "concept": "female_sexual_honor_status", "is_novel_concept": true, "register": "moral_religious", "crude": 5, "technical": 15, "recognition_pct_current": 85, "recognition_pct_peak": 85, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "A young woman considered sexually chaste and morally pure whose sharaf (honor) is intact and upheld through sexual modesty and adherence to family propriety norms.", "english_term": "woman of honor", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "Sharaf in this context specifically indexes female sexual purity; losing it typically entails loss of marriageability and family status. The phrase is a social designation rather than a legal one."}
{"term": "بنت الحلال", "lang": "ar", "script": "arab", "romanization": "bint al-ḥalāl", "category": "honor_shame_economy", "subcategory": "female_chastity", "concept": "halal_female_respectability", "is_novel_concept": true, "register": "moral_religious", "crude": 5, "technical": 10, "recognition_pct_current": 90, "recognition_pct_peak": 90, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "A respectable, chaste woman from a good family who conducts herself according to Islamic norms of sexual propriety; used as high praise indicating she is a lawful and appropriate marriage prospect.", "english_term": "respectable girl", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "Ḥalāl here means 'lawfully permissible'; the antonym 'bint ḥarām' (daughter of the forbidden) implies illegitimacy or sexual misconduct. The phrase endorses both sexual purity and social lineage simultaneously."}
{"term": "حرمة", "lang": "ar", "script": "arab", "romanization": "ḥurma", "category": "honor_shame_economy", "subcategory": "female_inviolability", "concept": "female_sexual_inviolability", "is_novel_concept": true, "register": "moral_religious", "crude": 5, "technical": 20, "recognition_pct_current": 80, "recognition_pct_peak": 80, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "The sacred sexual inviolability of a woman — especially a wife or female relative — encompassing her bodily sanctity, sexual honor, and the social prohibition against violating or disgracing her.", "english_term": "female inviolability", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "In colloquial dialects, ḥurma also simply means 'wife'; in MSA the root connotes both sacred prohibition and female personhood defined by sexual untouchability. Cognate with ḥarām (forbidden)."}
{"term": "شرفها بيدها", "lang": "ar", "script": "arab", "romanization": "sharafuhā biyadihā", "category": "honor_shame_economy", "subcategory": "female_honor_agency", "concept": "female_honor_self_responsibility", "is_novel_concept": true, "register": "moral_religious", "crude": 10, "technical": 5, "recognition_pct_current": 75, "recognition_pct_peak": 75, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Idiomatic phrase meaning a woman's sexual honor is in her own hands — she alone is responsible for maintaining or destroying it through her conduct.", "english_term": "her honor is in her own hands", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "Simultaneously empowering and constraining: it places sole moral responsibility on the woman for any sexual dishonor while implicitly absolving external actors (including perpetrators of coercion) of blame."}
{"term": "مشكوك في شرفها", "lang": "ar", "script": "arab", "romanization": "mashkūk fī sharafihā", "category": "honor_shame_economy", "subcategory": "honor_doubt", "concept": "female_honor_suspect", "is_novel_concept": true, "register": "moral_religious", "crude": 30, "technical": 10, "recognition_pct_current": 80, "recognition_pct_peak": 80, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "A woman whose sexual honor is publicly doubted or questioned; the social designation of a woman suspected of having engaged in unsanctioned sexual behavior.", "english_term": "woman of questionable virtue", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "Social consequences in honor-shame cultures can be severe: ostracism, forced marriage, or honor-based violence. The designation can arise from gossip, association, or actual conduct and is nearly impossible to formally rebut."}
{"term": "خروج المرأة", "lang": "ar", "script": "arab", "romanization": "khurūj al-marʾa", "category": "honor_shame_economy", "subcategory": "female_mobility", "concept": "female_public_mobility_gendered", "is_novel_concept": true, "register": "moral_religious", "crude": 5, "technical": 15, "recognition_pct_current": 70, "recognition_pct_peak": 70, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "A woman's departure from the domestic sphere into the public realm; in conservative Islamic discourse, her movement outside the home without a male guardian's permission carries strong implications for sexual propriety and family honor.", "english_term": "woman going out", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "Implicitly sexualized in conservative jurisprudence because public space is a site of fitna (sexual temptation/social chaos); a woman 'going out' risks both her own honor and her family's. The phrase appears in classical fiqh discussions of female travel and movement."}
{"term": "محتشمة", "lang": "ar", "script": "arab", "romanization": "muḥtashima", "category": "honor_shame_economy", "subcategory": "modesty", "concept": "sexual_conservatism", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "moral_religious", "crude": 0, "technical": 15, "recognition_pct_current": 90, "recognition_pct_peak": 90, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "A modest, chaste woman who adheres to norms of sexual decorum in dress and behavior; an adjective of high social praise in conservative Arab and Islamic contexts.", "english_term": "modest woman", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "From the root ḥ-sh-m, connoting shame, restraint, and decorum. Ḥishma is a cardinal social virtue distinct from Western prudishness in that it is actively valorized rather than mocked; the concept covers dress, gaze, speech, and bodily comportment."}
{"term": "وقحة", "lang": "ar", "script": "arab", "romanization": "waqiḥa", "category": "insults", "subcategory": "sexual_shamelessness", "concept": "wanton_behavior", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "slang_crude", "crude": 60, "technical": 5, "recognition_pct_current": 90, "recognition_pct_peak": 90, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "A shameless, brazen woman who violates norms of sexual modesty and decorum; used as an insult for a sexually forward or immodest woman who lacks ḥayāʾ.", "english_term": "shameless woman", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "The masculine form is waqiḥ; waqāḥa (shamelessness) applied to a woman is among the most damning insults in the honor-shame framework. The term is not inherently sexual in its root meaning but becomes strongly sexual when applied to women."}
{"term": "حياء زائد", "lang": "ar", "script": "arab", "romanization": "ḥayāʾ zāʾid", "category": "honor_shame_economy", "subcategory": "excessive_modesty", "concept": "prudishness", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "moral_religious", "crude": 5, "technical": 20, "recognition_pct_current": 65, "recognition_pct_peak": 65, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Excessive modesty or sexual inhibition beyond the culturally expected norm; describes a person so sexually reserved as to be unable to function normally in intimate or marital contexts.", "english_term": "excessive modesty", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "Ḥayāʾ (modesty/shame) is a cardinal Islamic virtue; calling it zāʾid (excessive) implies it has crossed from virtue into dysfunction, a distinction rarely drawn in traditional discourse. The phrase appears in contemporary marital counseling contexts more than classical texts."}
{"term": "إيلاء", "lang": "ar", "script": "arab", "romanization": "īlāʾ", "category": "religious_moral_status", "subcategory": "conjugal_oath", "concept": "marital_abstinence_oath", "is_novel_concept": true, "register": "classical_specialized", "crude": 10, "technical": 80, "recognition_pct_current": 35, "recognition_pct_peak": 35, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "A husband's oath to abstain from sexual intercourse with his wife for a period exceeding four months; in Islamic law, if he does not resume relations after that period the wife may seek a judicial divorce.", "english_term": "marital abstinence oath", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "Addressed in Quran 2:226–227; one of several classical mechanisms for marital dissolution whose trigger is explicitly sexual. Transliterated as 'ila' in Islamic studies literature. The four-month window is the maximum patience Islam grants a wife without recourse."}
{"term": "ظهار", "lang": "ar", "script": "arab", "romanization": "ẓihār", "category": "religious_moral_status", "subcategory": "prohibited_sexual_oath", "concept": "zihar_prohibition_oath", "is_novel_concept": true, "register": "classical_specialized", "crude": 20, "technical": 80, "recognition_pct_current": 30, "recognition_pct_peak": 30, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "A husband's pronouncement assimilating his wife to his mother's back, thereby rendering conjugal relations temporarily forbidden; in Islamic law it requires expiation (kafāra) before marital relations can resume.", "english_term": "zihar", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "A pre-Islamic Arabian divorce formula reinterpreted in Islam as a serious but reversible prohibition (Quran 58:2–4). The comparison to the mother's back evokes both a forbidden body part and the incest taboo; kafāra requires freeing a slave, fasting 60 days, or feeding 60 poor persons."}
{"term": "خلع", "lang": "ar", "script": "arab", "romanization": "khulaʿ", "category": "religious_moral_status", "subcategory": "wife_initiated_divorce", "concept": "wife_initiated_divorce", "is_novel_concept": true, "register": "classical_specialized", "crude": 5, "technical": 65, "recognition_pct_current": 65, "recognition_pct_peak": 65, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "A divorce initiated by the wife in which she returns her mahr or other compensation to the husband in exchange for release from the marriage contract and its conjugal obligations.", "english_term": "khul divorce", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "The word literally means 'removal/undressing'; conceptually the wife removes herself from the marriage garment. Sexual consent is central: khulʿ is most commonly sought to escape an unwanted sexual relationship. Considered a right in most madhhabs though procedural requirements vary."}
{"term": "نشوز", "lang": "ar", "script": "arab", "romanization": "nushūz", "category": "religious_moral_status", "subcategory": "conjugal_disobedience", "concept": "conjugal_disobedience", "is_novel_concept": true, "register": "classical_specialized", "crude": 20, "technical": 75, "recognition_pct_current": 50, "recognition_pct_peak": 50, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Marital recalcitrance in Islamic jurisprudence; specifically a wife's refusal of her husband's conjugal rights or a husband's failure to fulfill marital obligations, permitting a structured corrective response.", "english_term": "marital disobedience", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "Quran 4:34 prescribes a three-stage response to female nushūz (admonition, separate beds, then light physical correction); the sexual dimension is primary, as nushūz classically centers on refusal of the marital bed rather than other forms of disobedience."}
{"term": "حق الزوج", "lang": "ar", "script": "arab", "romanization": "ḥaqq al-zawj", "category": "religious_moral_status", "subcategory": "conjugal_right", "concept": "conjugal_rights_husband", "is_novel_concept": true, "register": "classical_specialized", "crude": 15, "technical": 60, "recognition_pct_current": 65, "recognition_pct_peak": 65, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "The husband's conjugal right; specifically the legally and religiously recognized entitlement to sexual access with his wife as part of the Islamic marriage contract.", "english_term": "husband's conjugal right", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "In classical fiqh the right is mutual but asymmetrical: the wife's corresponding ḥaqq exists but carries different legal weight and enforcement mechanisms. The concept is central to contemporary debates about the legal status of marital rape in Islamic jurisprudence."}
{"term": "طاعة زوجية", "lang": "ar", "script": "arab", "romanization": "ṭāʿa zawjiyya", "category": "religious_moral_status", "subcategory": "wifely_obedience", "concept": "wifely_sexual_obedience", "is_novel_concept": true, "register": "classical_specialized", "crude": 10, "technical": 55, "recognition_pct_current": 65, "recognition_pct_peak": 65, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Conjugal obedience; the religious and legal duty of a wife to be sexually available and obedient to her husband within the bounds of the Islamic marriage contract.", "english_term": "wifely obedience", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "Grounded in Quran 4:34 and multiple hadiths; classical scholars hold that a wife's refusal of ṭāʿa constitutes nushūz. Contemporary Islamic reformers contest whether the duty encompasses unconditional sexual availability or is constrained by the wife's consent and wellbeing."}
{"term": "عدّة", "lang": "ar", "script": "arab", "romanization": "ʿidda", "category": "religious_moral_status", "subcategory": "post_divorce_abstinence", "concept": "post_divorce_waiting_period", "is_novel_concept": true, "register": "classical_specialized", "crude": 5, "technical": 70, "recognition_pct_current": 75, "recognition_pct_peak": 75, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "The mandatory waiting period a Muslim woman must observe after divorce, widowhood, or annulment before she may remarry, requiring sexual abstinence and serving to establish the paternity of any pregnancy.", "english_term": "iddah", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "Duration varies: three menstrual cycles after revocable divorce, four months and ten days after widowhood, until childbirth if pregnant. Sexual abstinence is obligatory throughout; violation carries both religious and, in many jurisdictions, legal consequences."}
{"term": "رضاعة", "lang": "ar", "script": "arab", "romanization": "raḍāʿa", "category": "religious_moral_status", "subcategory": "milk_kinship", "concept": "milk_kinship", "is_novel_concept": true, "register": "classical_specialized", "crude": 5, "technical": 60, "recognition_pct_current": 65, "recognition_pct_peak": 65, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Ritual suckling or breastfeeding that in Islamic jurisprudence creates a kinship bond between the nursing woman and the infant, generating sexual prohibitions equivalent to those of blood kinship.", "english_term": "milk kinship", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "Quran 4:23 and multiple hadiths establish raḍāʿa as a category of sexual prohibition (maḥārim). Classical threshold is five or more confirmed sucklings in the first two years of life; the resulting sexual prohibitions are legally equivalent to those from blood relation."}
{"term": "تحريم بالرضاعة", "lang": "ar", "script": "arab", "romanization": "taḥrīm bil-raḍāʿa", "category": "religious_moral_status", "subcategory": "milk_kinship_prohibition", "concept": "milk_kinship_marriage_prohibition", "is_novel_concept": true, "register": "classical_specialized", "crude": 5, "technical": 75, "recognition_pct_current": 50, "recognition_pct_peak": 50, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "The Islamic legal prohibition on marriage and sexual relations arising from shared nursing; the specific subset of forbidden sexual relationships created by the breastfeeding bond rather than blood kinship.", "english_term": "milk kinship marriage prohibition", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "Explicitly enumerated in Quran 4:23 alongside blood relatives. A notable contemporary application: Saudi scholars raised concern about hospital breast milk banks creating unknown milk-kinship bonds that could unknowingly prevent future marriages between recipients."}
{"term": "مُحرَّمة", "lang": "ar", "script": "arab", "romanization": "muḥarrama", "category": "religious_moral_status", "subcategory": "forbidden_woman", "concept": "sexually_forbidden_woman", "is_novel_concept": true, "register": "moral_religious", "crude": 10, "technical": 50, "recognition_pct_current": 70, "recognition_pct_peak": 70, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "A woman who is sexually forbidden to a man by Islamic law, whether through blood kinship, milk kinship, active marriage ties, or other Quranic prohibition categories.", "english_term": "forbidden woman", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "The Quranic term for categories of women a man may not marry or have sexual relations with (Quran 4:23). Distinct from ḥarām as a generic prohibition — muḥarrama specifically designates a person who is categorically off-limits as a sexual or marital partner."}
{"term": "محلّلة", "lang": "ar", "script": "arab", "romanization": "muḥallala", "category": "religious_moral_status", "subcategory": "tahleel_intermediate_wife", "concept": "tahleel_woman", "is_novel_concept": true, "register": "classical_specialized", "crude": 35, "technical": 65, "recognition_pct_current": 40, "recognition_pct_peak": 40, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "A woman who serves as the intermediate wife in a tahleel arrangement, marrying and sexually consummating with a man solely to render his triply-divorced ex-wife lawful for remarriage to him; the role is widely condemned as a legal fiction.", "english_term": "tahleel woman", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "Literally 'the one made lawful'; her sexual consummation with the intermediate husband is the required condition rendering the first wife licit again. A hadith explicitly curses the muḥallil (intermediate husband) and those who arrange such marriages."}
{"term": "زواج المحلل", "lang": "ar", "script": "arab", "romanization": "zawāj al-muḥallil", "category": "religious_moral_status", "subcategory": "tahleel_marriage", "concept": "tahleel_marriage", "is_novel_concept": true, "register": "classical_specialized", "crude": 35, "technical": 70, "recognition_pct_current": 50, "recognition_pct_peak": 50, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "A marriage contracted specifically to allow a triply-divorced woman to return to her first husband; requires consummation and subsequent divorce from the intermediate husband before the original couple may remarry.", "english_term": "tahleel marriage", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "Sexual consummation (al-wuṭʾ) is non-negotiable: without it the intermediate marriage does not achieve its purpose. Hanbali and most Shafi'i scholars consider it absolutely prohibited; Hanafi school permits it if the second marriage is ostensibly genuine in intent."}
{"term": "كفاءة النكاح", "lang": "ar", "script": "arab", "romanization": "kafāʾat al-nikāḥ", "category": "religious_moral_status", "subcategory": "marital_equivalence", "concept": "marital_equivalence_islamic", "is_novel_concept": true, "register": "classical_specialized", "crude": 5, "technical": 75, "recognition_pct_current": 35, "recognition_pct_peak": 35, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "The Islamic jurisprudential requirement of compatibility or equivalence between spouses in religion, lineage, and sometimes occupation or wealth, regarded as a condition for a valid and socially endorsed marriage.", "english_term": "matrimonial equivalence", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "Primarily invoked when a marriage is contracted without the guardian's (walī's) consent: a groom of lower kafāʾa gives the walī grounds for annulment. The concept intersects with sexuality insofar as mismatched status is seen as risking the woman's honor and the family's social standing."}
{"term": "مهر", "lang": "ar", "script": "arab", "romanization": "mahr", "category": "religious_moral_status", "subcategory": "marriage_dower", "concept": "islamic_marriage_dower", "is_novel_concept": true, "register": "classical_specialized", "crude": 5, "technical": 50, "recognition_pct_current": 85, "recognition_pct_peak": 85, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "The obligatory gift of money or property given by a groom to his bride as a condition of the Islamic marriage contract, becoming her exclusive property and legally tied to the sexual consummation of the marriage.", "english_term": "mahr", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "If divorce occurs before consummation the bride receives only half the specified mahr; full mahr is due upon consummation, explicitly linking the dower to sexual access. This makes mahr the legal expression of the sexual contract within Islamic marriage."}
{"term": "شبق الزوج", "lang": "ar", "script": "arab", "romanization": "shabaq al-zawj", "category": "desire_arousal_descriptors", "subcategory": "marital_lust", "concept": "lust", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "moral_religious", "crude": 30, "technical": 20, "recognition_pct_current": 60, "recognition_pct_peak": 60, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "The intense sexual desire or lust of a husband; in Islamic jurisprudential discourse, the husband's sexual appetite that generates the wife's legal duty of availability and underpins the conjugal-rights framework.", "english_term": "husband's lust", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "Shabaq (شبق) denotes intense, urgent sexual craving; in this marital-juridical compound the husband's desire is not condemned but recognized as a natural drive that Islamic law structures around, requiring the wife's sexual availability as the legally responsive mechanism."}
{"term": "امتناع الزوجة", "lang": "ar", "script": "arab", "romanization": "imtināʿ al-zawja", "category": "religious_moral_status", "subcategory": "conjugal_refusal", "concept": "wifely_conjugal_refusal", "is_novel_concept": true, "register": "classical_specialized", "crude": 15, "technical": 55, "recognition_pct_current": 55, "recognition_pct_peak": 55, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "A wife's withholding of conjugal relations from her husband; in Islamic jurisprudence, sustained refusal without a legitimate excuse constitutes nushūz and may entitle the husband to withhold maintenance and take corrective measures.", "english_term": "wifely conjugal refusal", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "Legitimate excuses for refusal include illness, menstruation, and prescribed fasting days; refusal on other grounds is generally treated as a legal and religious breach. The term sits at the intersection of sexuality, marital law, and the distribution of power within Islamic marriage."}
